  "items": [
    {
      "id": "m1",
      "num": 1,
      "level": "A1",
      "title": "The Turkish alphabet & first words",
      "subtitle": "Pronounce all 29 letters and greet like a local",
      "objectifs": [
        "Know the 29 letters of the Turkish alphabet",
        "Pronounce ç, ş, ğ, ı, ö, ü correctly",
        "Read any Turkish word out loud",
        "Greet people and say thank you"
      ],
      "blocks": [
        {
          "t": "p",
          "text": "Good news: Turkish is a <b>phonetic</b> language. Every letter is always pronounced the same way, with no exceptions. Once you have mastered the alphabet, you can read any word, even without understanding it. The Latin alphabet has been used since 1928, following the great letter reform introduced by <b>Atatürk</b>."
        },
        {
          "t": "h",
          "text": "The 8 vowels"
        },
        {
          "t": "p",
          "text": "Turkish has 8 vowels. Four will feel familiar (a, e, i as in Spanish, o); four take a little effort."
        },
        {
          "t": "table",
          "caption": "The Turkish vowels",
          "head": [
            "Letter",
            "Pronunciation (approximate)",
            "Example"
          ],
          "rows": [
            [
              "a / e / i / o",
              "a, e as in « bed », i (short and crisp), o",
              "<tur>elma</tur> (apple)"
            ],
            [
              "u",
              "oo as in « boot »",
              "<tur>okul</tur> (school)"
            ],
            [
              "ı (dotless i)",
              "a short, unrounded « uh », as in the word « the »",
              "<tur>ışık</tur> (light)"
            ],
            [
              "ü",
              "the French « u » (as in « rue »)",
              "<tur>gül</tur> (rose)"
            ],
            [
              "ö",
              "the French « eu » (as in « peu »)",
              "<tur>göz</tur> (eye)"
            ]
          ]
        },
        {
          "t": "note",
          "kind": "tip",
          "tag": "Tip",
          "text": "Watch out for the classic trap: <b>ı</b> (capital I, no dot) and <b>i</b> (capital İ, with a dot) are two different letters. <tur>kedi</tur> (cat) ≠ <tur>kırmızı</tur> (red)."
        },
        {
          "t": "h",
          "text": "The consonants that change everything"
        },
        {
          "t": "table",
          "caption": "Specific consonants",
          "head": [
            "Letter",
            "Pronounced",
            "Example"
          ],
          "rows": [
            [
              "ç",
              "« ch » as in « chair »",
              "<tur>çay</tur> (tea): chai"
            ],
            [
              "ş",
              "« sh » as in « ship »",
              "<tur>şehir</tur> (city): sheh-heer"
            ],
            [
              "c",
              "« j » as in « jam »",
              "<tur>cam</tur> (glass): jam"
            ],
            [
              "j",
              "« zh » as in « measure » (rare)",
              "<tur>jilet</tur> (razor)"
            ],
            [
              "ğ",
              "barely pronounced: it lengthens the preceding vowel",
              "<tur>dağ</tur> (mountain): « daa »"
            ],
            [
              "h",
              "always aspirated, as in English",
              "<tur>hava</tur> (air, weather)"
            ]
          ]
        },
        {
          "t": "p",
          "text": "The other letters are pronounced roughly as in English, with two small differences: <b>r</b> is rolled, and <b>v</b> is sometimes softer, between « v » and « w ». The <b>y</b> sounds like the « y » in « yogurt »."
        },
        {
          "t": "h",
          "text": "Read out loud"
        },
        {
          "t": "ex",
          "items": [
            {
              "tr": "Merhaba",
              "fr": "Hello"
            },
            {
              "tr": "teşekkür ederim",
              "fr": "Thank you"
            },
            {
              "tr": "günaydın",
              "fr": "Good morning"
            },
            {
              "tr": "nasılsın?",
              "fr": "How are you?"
            },
            {
              "tr": "çok güzel",
              "fr": "Very beautiful / very good"
            },
            {
              "tr": "hoş geldiniz",
              "fr": "Welcome"
            }
          ]
        },
        {
          "t": "note",
          "kind": "tip",
          "tag": "Method",
          "text": "Listen to each word with the speaker button, then repeat it out loud, copying the rhythm. Turkish has final stress: the stress almost always falls on the <b>last syllable</b>: merhaba (merha-<b>BA</b>), güzel (gu-<b>ZEL</b>), teşekkür (te-şek-<b>KÜR</b>)."
        }
      ],
      "dialogue": {
        "title": "First meeting",
        "lines": [
          {
            "name": "Elif",
            "tr": "Merhaba!",
            "fr": "Hello!"
          },
          {
            "name": "Magloire",
            "tr": "Merhaba, nasılsın?",
            "fr": "Hello, how are you?"
          },
          {
            "name": "Elif",
            "tr": "İyiyim, teşekkür ederim. Sen nasılsın?",
            "fr": "I'm fine, thank you. And you?"
          },
          {
            "name": "Magloire",
            "tr": "Ben de iyiyim. Adın ne?",
            "fr": "I'm fine too. What's your name?"
          },
          {
            "name": "Elif",
            "tr": "Benim adım Elif. Sen?",
            "fr": "My name is Elif. And you?"
          },
          {
            "name": "Magloire",
            "tr": "Ben Magloire. Memnun oldum!",
            "fr": "I'm Magloire. Nice to meet you!"
          },
          {
            "name": "Elif",
            "tr": "Ben de memnun oldum. Görüşürüz!",
            "fr": "Nice to meet you too. See you soon!"
          }
        ]
      },
      "vocab": [
