      ],
      "quiz": [
        {
          "q": "How is the letter « ş » pronounced?",
          "opts": [
            "« sh » as in « ship »",
            "« ch » as in « cheese »",
            "a hissing « s »",
            "the French « j »"
          ],
          "a": 0,
          "exp": "ş is pronounced like « sh » in « ship »: şehir = sheh-heer."
        },
        {
          "q": "How many letters does the Turkish alphabet have?",
          "opts": [
            "26",
            "29",
            "32",
            "24"
          ],
          "a": 1,
          "exp": "29 letters: the Latin alphabet minus q, w and x, plus ç, ğ, ı, ö, ş and ü."
        },
        {
          "q": "The letter « ğ » (soft g)…",
          "opts": [
            "is pronounced like a hard « g »",
            "lengthens the vowel that comes before it",
            "is no longer written today",
            "is pronounced « y »"
          ],
          "a": 1,
          "exp": "dağ is pronounced « daa »: ğ is never a hard g; it stretches the vowel."
        },
        {
          "q": "How is « ı » (dotless i) pronounced?",
          "opts": [
            "like the « ee » in « see »",
            "like a short, unrounded « uh »",
            "like « y »",
            "it is silent"
          ],
          "a": 1,
          "exp": "The letter Iı resembles the « e » in « the »: ışık (light) is pronounced « uh-shuk »."
        },
        {
          "q": "What does « teşekkür ederim » mean?",
          "opts": [
            "Goodbye",
            "Please",
            "Thank you",
            "Excuse me"
          ],
          "a": 2,
          "exp": "teşekkür ederim = I thank you. Short informal form: sağ ol."
        }
      ]
    },
    {
      "id": "m2",
      "num": 2,
      "level": "A1",
      "title": "Introducing yourself: I am…",
      "subtitle": "There is no verb « to be »: it attaches to the end of the word",
      "objectifs": [
        "Say your name, your nationality, your job",
        "Understand the logic of personal suffixes",
        "Ask « and you? »"
      ],
      "blocks": [
        {
          "t": "p",
          "text": "Surprise: in Turkish, <b>there is no verb « to be » in the present tense</b>. To say « I am a student », you attach a small suffix to the end of the noun: <tur>öğrenci</tur> + <b>-im</b> → <tur>öğrenciyim</tur>. The pronoun (ben, sen…) is optional: the suffix already says who is speaking."
        },
        {
          "t": "h",
          "text": "Conjugating « to be »"
        },
        {
          "t": "table",
          "caption": "Personal suffixes for « to be »",
          "head": [
            "Pronoun",
            "Suffix",
            "Example",
            "Translation"
          ],
          "rows": [
            [
              "ben (I)",
              "-im / -ım / -üm / -um",
              "<tur>öğretmenim</tur>",
              "I am a teacher"
            ],
            [
              "sen (you, informal)",
              "-sin / -sın / -sün / -sun",
              "<tur>öğrencisin</tur>",
              "you are a student"
            ],
            [
              "o (he/she)",
              "Ø",
              "<tur>doktor</tur>",
              "he/she is a doctor"
            ],
            [
              "biz (we)",
              "-iz / -ız / -üz / -uz",
              "<tur>Türküz</tur>",
              "we are Turkish"
            ],
            [
              "siz (you, formal)",
              "-siniz / -sınız / -sünüz / -sunuz",
              "<tur>yorgunsunuz</tur>",
              "you are tired"
            ],
            [
              "onlar (they)",
              "Ø (or -ler)",
              "<tur>öğretmen</tur>",
              "they are teachers"
            ]
          ]
        },
        {
          "t": "note",
          "kind": "info",
          "tag": "Why 4 forms?",
          "text": "-im, -ım, -üm, -um… the suffix adapts to the last vowel of the word. This is <b>vowel harmony</b>, covered in module 4. For now, learn each word as a single block."
        },
        {
          "t": "h",
          "text": "Your first name"
        },
        {
          "t": "p",
          "text": "Two ways to say your name: <tur>Benim adım Elif</tur> (my name is Elif: literally « ad-ım ») or, more simply, <tur>Ben Elif</tur> (me, [it's] Elif). To ask: <tur>Adın ne?</tur> (informal) / <tur>Adınız ne?</tur> (formal)."
        },
        {
          "t": "h",
          "text": "Nationalities & jobs"
        },
        {
          "t": "ex",
          "items": [
            {
              "tr": "Ben Magloire. Fransızım.",
              "fr": "My name is Magloire. I'm French."
            },
            {
              "tr": "Ben Türküm.",
              "fr": "I'm Turkish."
            },
            {
              "tr": "Öğretmenim.",
              "fr": "I'm a teacher."
            },
            {
              "tr": "Mühendisim.",
              "fr": "I'm an engineer."
            },
            {
              "tr": "Öğrenci misin?",
              "fr": "Are you a student?"
            },
            {
              "tr": "Evet, öğrenciyim.",
              "fr": "Yes, I'm a student."
            }
          ]
        },
        {
          "t": "note",
          "kind": "tip",
          "tag": "Culture",
          "text": "The question <tur>nerelisin?</tur> means « where are you from? ». Natural answers: <tur>Fransızım</tur> (I'm French) or <tur>Fransa'dan geliyorum</tur> (I come from France)."
        }
      ],
      "dialogue": {
        "title": "At the university cafeteria",
        "lines": [
          {
            "name": "Magloire",
            "tr": "Merhaba! Burada oturabilir miyim?",
            "fr": "Hello! May I sit here?"
          },
